## Observability

| | |
|---|---|
| New Relic agent jar | {{ 'present' if parity_newrelic.stat.exists else '**ABSENT**' }} |
| Agent actually reporting | {% if 'connected to collector' in (parity_nr_connect.stdout | default('')) %}yes — `{{ parity_nr_connect.stdout }}`{% else %}**NO — {{ parity_nr_connect.stdout | default('no evidence in the journal') }}**{% endif %} |
| App name | `{{ newrelic_app_name | default('unset') }}` |
| Environment | `{{ newrelic_environment | default('unset') }}` |
| Application logging | {{ newrelic_application_logging_enabled | default('unset') }} |
| Distributed tracing | {{ newrelic_distributed_tracing_enabled | default('unset') }} |

The agent is required for **parity**, not only for metrics: production runs it
and it instruments the JVM at a cost. A rig without it is measurably faster than
production for a reason unrelated to the code under test.

**The jar being present is not evidence of reporting**, which is why both rows
are here. That distinction is not theoretical: this environment ran with the
agent attached, logging its own application name, and reaching New Relic not at
all — the licence key was a User API key rather than an ingest key, and the
agent failed with `UnknownHostException` against a collector hostname derived
from it. A report checking only the jar called that "attached".

## Co-tenant workloads

| | |
|---|---|
| ETL hosts in this environment | {{ parity_etl_hosts }} |

{% if parity_etl_hosts | int == 0 %}
**No ETL host.** Results describe sync with the database to itself. ETL runs a
90-minute Quartz cycle that reads `public` in competition with sync, writes org
schemas and rebuilds materialised views, all against the same fixed 3,000 IOPS —
so an environment without it is quieter than any real one. The number the
hosting decision needs is the *delta* between the two, which cannot be measured
from this configuration alone.
{% endif %}

## Still to be recorded per run, by hand

These cannot be read off the host and belong beside the results:

- **Tenancy model** — shared or dedicated. Results are not comparable across models.
- **Index state** — post-load indexes are pristine; production's carry accumulated bloat.
- **Injector position and address** — a sync is ~109 requests, so 16 ms of round trip is 1.74 s on a 14.1 s median. A constant offset, not noise.
- **Whether a concurrent ETL cycle ran**, once an ETL host exists.
- **Dataset identity** — generator SHA and org config revision, per the baseline snapshot's tags.
